  "problem": "A certain company produces three types of ground coffee: fine, medium, and coarse. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of ground coffee each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Fine** | **Medium** | **Coarse** |\n| To: | **Fine** | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Medium** | $0.3$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Coarse** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of fine ground coffee customers switch to medium ground coffee each year, and $20%$ of coarse ground coffee customers switch to fine ground coffee.\n\nCurrently, $300\\,000$ customers buy fine ground coffee, $500\\,000$ customers buy medium ground coffee, and $200\\,000$ customers buy coarse ground coffee. How many customers will purchase fine ground coffee in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change.",
